{"text": "A Summary of Facts Concerning Smoking, Lung Cancer, and Nutrition\nBy\nH. H, Boynton\nIntroduction\nAt a time when most forms of cancer are declining, the incidence of epithelial cancers\ncontinues to rise. Cancer of the colon, prostate, and bladder are up about 20%, Far\nmore serious, however, is lung cancer with an increase of 125% according to Time Maga-\nzine (9/15/75) quoting from American Cancer Society statistics.\nCan Lung cancer be prevented? A careful analysis of research data and scientiic re-\nports from several nations strongly suggests that the answer to this question is \"yes\", It\nis probable that the incidence of lung cancer, even among\u2019 people who smoke \u2018cigarettes can\nbe significantly reduced through a simple, risk-free, inexpensive nutritional regimen.\n\nNutrition and Lung Function :\n_,_,t is well known that Vitamin A is essential to normal lung function. A deficiency pro-\nduces a condition called squamous metaplasia (1) (2) (3) in which specialized ciliated colum-\nnar cells and goblet cells fail toform. They are replaced by dry, keratinized, non-func-\ntioning tissue. Ciliated cells are analogous to tiny brooms which beat rhythmically to\nsweep inhaled pollutants, bacteria, and carcinogens upward and out of the lungs. Goblet\ncells secrete mucous which forms a sticky \"blanket\" on top of the cilia which traps for-\neign matter to be borne away by the cilia. There can be no doubt that this system is mar-\nvelously effective. We all inhale prodigiously large amounts of particulate matter during\nour lifetimes which is efficiently cleansed trom our lungs. The normal lung is populated\nwith many millions of these highly specialized eetls.\n\nA deficiency of Vitamin A also causes \"basal cell hyperplasia\u201d which is simply a pro\n\nliferation of the cells directly below the cilia and goblet cells. Among people who smoke\n\nCOPYRIGHT (2) 1975\nNUTRITION 21, LA iUiLA, CRU.\n\n2S64L09Zz00T", "words": [{"text": "A", "x0": 16